Race condition. Multiple requests read same value before any write commit.

## Fix: Atomic Update

```sql
-- Bad: read then write (two trips, race window)
SELECT counter FROM table WHERE id = $1;
UPDATE table SET counter = $2 WHERE id = $1;

-- Good: atomic single statement
UPDATE table
SET counter = counter + 1
WHERE id = $1
RETURNING counter;
```

One DB roundtrip. No race window. DB serialize writes to same row.

## Node.js

```js
// Bad
const { rows: [row] } = await pool.query('SELECT counter FROM t WHERE id=$1', [id]);
await pool.query('UPDATE t SET counter=$1 WHERE id=$2', [row.counter + 1, id]);
res.json({ counter: row.counter + 1 }); // stale if concurrent

// Good
const { rows: [row] } = await pool.query(
  'UPDATE t SET counter = counter + 1 WHERE id = $1 RETURNING counter',
  [id]
);
res.json({ counter: row.counter }); // always fresh, always unique
```

## If Multi-Step Logic Required

Wrap in transaction + row lock:

```js
const client = await pool.connect();
try {
  await client.query('BEGIN');
  const { rows: [row] } = await client.query(
    'SELECT counter FROM t WHERE id = $1 FOR UPDATE', // locks row
    [id]
  );
  // safe to do complex logic here — no other tx can touch this row
  const next = myComplexLogic(row.counter);
  await client.query('UPDATE t SET counter=$1 WHERE id=$2', [next, id]);
  await client.query('COMMIT');
  res.json({ counter: next });
} catch (e) {
  await client.query('ROLLBACK');
  throw e;
} finally {
  client.release();
}
```

`FOR UPDATE` block other transactions until this one commit.

## Pick Right Fix

| Situation | Fix |
|-----------|-----|
| Simple increment | `UPDATE ... SET x = x+1 RETURNING x` |
| Complex logic on value | Transaction + `FOR UPDATE` |
| Cross-table atomicity | Transaction + `FOR UPDATE` |

Atomic update cover 90% of cases. Use transaction only when logic too complex for single SQL.
